  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe develop a multi-resolution Bayesian optimization algorithm that adaptively explores both continuous and combinatorial search spaces to minimize the maximum shift-overlap integral. The central idea is to use Bayesian optimization (Gaussian processes) to guide the search for configurations that minimize the overlap, leveraging the fact that the objective function is expensive to evaluate but potentially smooth. We also combine this with a carefully designed heuristic initialization based on the Fourier transform of the initial solution, addressing the high dimensionality and non-convexity of the problem.\n\nUnlike previous approaches that relied on combinatorial perturbations or simple gradient-based methods, our strategy uses probabilistic modeling to balance exploration and exploitation. The algorithm is decomposed into three main phases: Bayesian optimization-guided search, combined with multi-scale evaluation and adaptive sampling. This allows escaping local optima more effectively and finding globally optimal configurations by transferring solutions between differing resolutions while maintaining the integral constraint exactly.\n\nLimitations: Bayesian optimization is computationally intensive due to the repeated correlation calculations. The greedy Heuristic initialization may lead to suboptimal amounts, but the adaptive redundancy ensures consistency. The method is sensitive to length scale choices in the Gaussian process kernel.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent the unknown function h(x) with N samples over [0,2]. Let x_i = i * dx, i=0,...,N-1 with dx=2/(N).\n\nObjective:\nFind h such that min(max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx]) \n= min(max_k [max_{k\u2208Z} (1/N) * maxcorr] )\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\u2211_{i=0}^{N-1} h_i = N/2 (integral constraint)\n\nCore component: We model the true objective function f(h) as a Gaussian process, where h is a N-dimensional vector. However, due to functional dependence and bumpy values, we take advantage of Nystr\u00f6m extension may not apply directly.\n\nObjective Measurement:\nThe measurement we maximize depends on a correlation term:\nf_k(h) = \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nbut we use the finite difference approximation: \nf\u0302(h) = max_k { (1/2N) * |<h, \u0125|} where \u0125(x) = 1 - h(x mod 2)\n\nWe use a GPU-accelerated implementation by truncating the Hamiltonian simulations to single precision.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe algorithm begins by generating an initial N_candidates candidates by Fourier transforming the given parent solution. We use a brute-force random initialization as fallback. \n\n(Continued in the full strategy response)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nWe implement a three-phase algorithm:\n\nPhase A: Initialization\n- I5NS1.4MLE-SOEx: Initial heuristic based on Fourier transform + random restarts\n- I5NS1.4MLE-SOExxy: Diversified copies for context\n\nPhase B: Iterated Filtering with Bayesian Learning\n- Primary operation: GP model built on Python kernel\n- Secondary: NOX judiciously selecting from top 5 candidates\n\nPhase C:Gradient-based fine-tuning\n- Method: Bayesian optimization using scipy.optimize\n- Objective: max-k correlation\n- Feasibility: Integral projected via linear programming\n\nDetail diverges here due to space limits.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nExecution is broken into phases:\n- 0-10%: Validation and residual constraint checking\n- 10-460%: GP-guided search at current resolution\n- 460-520%: Resolution transfer to finer/coarser grids\n- 520-640%: Multi-start local optimization\n- 640-770%: Cross-scale comparison and hybrid techniques\n- 770-920%: Bayesian improvement cycles\n- 920-1100%: Final polishing and compact representation\n\nEach phase includes rigorous time monitoring with sub-budgets to prevent overflow.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nCandidates must pass at least one of the following tests:\n- Finite valued objective\n- Integral constraint tolerance \u2264\u03b3\n- Value stays between 0 and 1\n- Sensible variance structure at wake scale\n\nReturned value is the best configuration found, along with its objective and resolution. The function will automatically project to feasibility boundaries.",
